{
  "abstract": "Each player independently keeps a share of one secret qubit with probability p. A false approval occurs when Ganguly's circuit rule approves the surviving group and it cannot recover the qubit. Among finite connected simple graphs with one designated dealer and a nonempty, pairwise-intersecting family of approved groups, the supremum of this probability is zero at p = 0 and p = 1, equals p for 0 < p ≤ 1/2, and equals one for 1/2 < p < 1.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Robert Leshner"
    }
  ],
